Q: Get a value of non-default-constructible type `T` from a byte array without undefined behavior A: <p>[expr.reinterpret.cast]p7 defines the cast as equivalent to two <code>static_cast</code>s (via <code>void*</code>). It also contains a note saying that casting to some other type and back yields the original value if the intermediate type doesn't have stricter alignment requirements. But notes are not normative.</p>
<p>[expr.static.cast]p12 defines the cast from <code>void*</code> to <code>T*</code>, saying that if the <code>void*</code> points to a byte that starts a memory region containing a <code>T</code>, the cast yields a pointer to this <code>T</code>, but otherwise (unless alignment interferes) it yields the pointer value unchanged.</p>
<p>[conv.ptr]p2 defines the conversion from <code>T*</code> to <code>void*</code> that is invoked by the other <code>static_cast</code>. It yields the pointer value unchanged.</p>
<p>So if you go from <code>T*</code> to <code>byte*</code> and back, you get the following sequence of conversions:</p>
<pre><code>auto tmp1 = static_cast&lt;void*&gt;(ptr_in);
// to void*, unchanged pointer value

auto ptr_bytes = static_cast&lt;byte*&gt;(tmp1);
// not higher aligned, not pointer-compatible, unchanged pointer value

auto tmp2 = static_cast&lt;void*&gt;(ptr_bytes);
// to void*, unchanged pointer value

auto ptr_out = static_cast&lt;T*&gt;(tmp2);
// since pointer value is unchanged since ptr_in, pointer points to a
// T object, so cast yields pointer to this object
</code></pre>
